A harsh systolic ejection murmur maximal at the left 2nd-3rd intercostal space / left upper sternal border, radiating up toward the left clavicle/neck, with an associated palpable thrill and clear augmentation with inspiration is a right-sided ejection murmur.

Right sided murmurs are augmented by inspiration because inspiratory fall in intrathoracic pressure augments right ventricular filling and stroke volume across the pulmonary outflow tract. A thrill implies a significant pressure gradient across the outflow tract rather than a soft flow murmur. The clinical picture of progressive exertional dyspnoea, fatigue, lightheadedness in a 31 y woman with a history of faint childhood cyanosis and ECG evidence of right ventricular hypertrophy is the natural history of chronic right ventricular pressure overload from a congenital right ventricular outflow obstruction, i.e. valvular pulmonic stenosis / right ventricular outflow obstruction.

Valvular pulmonic stenosis/right ventricular outflow obstruction is not offered in A-G.

* Ebstein anomaly = holosystolic tricuspid regurgitation at the left lower sternal border with marked cyanosis;
* Patent ductus arteriosus = continuous machinery murmur;
* Mitral valve prolapse = mid/late systolic click ± late systolic murmur at the apex;
* HCM with LVOT obstruction = dynamic left-sided outflow murmur at the left lower sternal border ↑ with Valsalva, ↓ with inspiration;
* Tricuspid stenosis = diastolic rumble;
* VSD = harsh holosystolic murmur best at the left lower sternal border.

A secundum atrial septal defect can give an increased pulmonary flow systolic ejection murmur at the left upper sternal border that is inspiratory augmented and is associated with RV volume overload/RVH, and that is the classic teaching mimic of the pulmonary ejection murmur described here. The left axis deviation with RVH that is quoted is most in keeping with an ostium primum/AV canal type ASD with left anterior fascicular block, which is why D is repeatedly forced as the exam answer.

Strictly pathophysiologically the syndrome described is congenital pulmonic stenosis/right ventricular outflow obstruction. That entity is not represented in A-G, so the correct choice as written is H. None of these choices. If a choice must be forced from the list the pulmonary flow murmur most commonly taught in this location is the increased flow murmur of an atrial septal defect, D, with the ECG best fitting a primum/AV canal phenotype.

