7.text:
PUBLIC RESPONSE: This is a right-sided systolic ejection murmur and the localization is pulmonary.

Maximal at the left upper sternal border/2nd-3rd ICS, radiating toward the left clavicle/neck, systolic ejection quality, augmented by inspiration with a palpable thrill over the pulmonary area is the classic pulmonary area ejection murmur. Inspiration lowers intrathoracic pressure, increases venous return and RV filling/stroke volume, so right-sided murmurs get louder with inspiration whereas left-sided murmurs diminish.

Acoustically that can be produced by increased flow across the pulmonary valve with chronic pulmonary overcirculation. A hemodynamically significant left-to-right interatrial shunt raises pulmonary blood flow, produces a pulmonary area systolic ejection murmur that is inspiratory augmented, and when the shunt is large the flow can be harsh enough for a palpable thrill. Over time you get right ventricular volume overload with secondary pressure overload, RVH on ECG, and progressive exertional dyspnea, fatigue and lightheadedness in a young adult. Faint childhood cyanosis fits a large interatrial left-to-right shunt with intermittent right-to-left equalisation under stress, fever or raised pulmonary vascular resistance.

The ECG is the discriminator that makes this an ASD in the list. Isolated valvular pulmonic stenosis would give RVH with a rightward frontal axis. Here we have left axis deviation with signs of RVH. That pattern is taught for an ostium primum / AV canal ASD with left anterior fascicular block and altered frontal plane forces from right heart enlargement. Primum/AV canal ASD reconciles a pulmonary area inspiratory-augmented systolic ejection murmur with thrill, RVH and left axis deviation in one lesion and explains late presentation in the 3rd decade after decades of tolerated left-to-right shunting.

The other listed entities do not reproduce a LUSB inspiratory-augmented systolic ejection murmur with thrill and RVH:
Ebstein anomaly = holosystolic tricuspid regurgitation at the left lower sternal border;
PDA = continuous machinery;
Mitral valve prolapse = mid/late systolic click ± late systolic murmur at the apex;
HCM with LVOT obstruction = left lower sternal border dynamic murmur increased by Valsalva and decreased by inspiration;
Tricuspid stenosis = diastolic rumble;
VSD = harsh holosystolic murmur best at the left lower sternal border.

Yes, valvular pulmonic stenosis is the real world acoustic mimic for this murmur and is why some are leaning to H. It is not in A-G. Among the choices provided the only lesion that generates a pulmonary area systolic ejection murmur that is inspiratory augmented, can be harsh enough for a thrill, is associated with RVH and can present with left axis deviation in its primum/AV canal form is an atrial septal defect.

That is the intended teaching mapping for this pulmonary flow murmur: D. Atrial septal defect, best thought of as ostium primum / AV canal type given the left axis deviation + RVH.

